Certainly one of my favorite Lily episodes in season one.
绝对是第一季里我最喜欢的莉莉单集之一。

00:08:23.279 --> 00:08:30.360
I would argue this is the first time we really do like an episode where like Lily really gets to carry it.
我甚至觉得，这是我们第一次真正拍出那种让莉莉真正挑大梁的单集。

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.240
I just feel like this is her episode.
我就是觉得这就是她的专属单集。

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.000
I say that and then of course, there's so many great moments leading up to this.
话虽如此，当然了，前面还有很多非常精彩的铺垫。

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:42.279
But this, I really feel like she is the emotional core of this episode, maybe in a way we hadn't done as much to in those first age before this.
但这一集，我真的觉得她是整集的情感核心，或许在此之前的前八集里，我们还没怎么展现过这一点。

00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:43.200
And I love it.
我太喜欢了。

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.759
She's, Hallie Hannigan, man, she's good.
她，哈莉·汉尼根（Hallie Hannigan），天哪，她太棒了。

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:47.600
She's good at acting.
她演技很好。

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.519
We've established that, but we'll say it again.
我们之前已经确认过了，不过还是要再说一遍。

00:08:49.519 --> 00:08:50.360
She's really good.
她真的很棒。

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:51.200
We'll say it again.
我们再夸一遍。

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:52.039
She's good.
她就是很棒。

00:08:52.039 --> 00:09:01.000
It also kind of illustrates like, you know, like US politics is so insanely fractured.
这其实也说明了，你知道的，比如美国政治是多么疯狂地撕裂。

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:05.440
And it's kind of like, if you really look at America, it's like Europe.
这就有点像，如果你真的审视美国，它就像欧洲。

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.440
It's a collection of different countries that are like smashed together.
它是一堆被硬生生拼凑在一起的不同国家。

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:18.519
Like Minnesota is different than New York City, is different than Utah, Ohio, California, like Texas, like they're all these different cultures.
比如明尼苏达州不同于纽约市，不同于犹他州、俄亥俄州、加利福尼亚州、得克萨斯州，它们都有着完全不同的文化。

00:09:18.519 --> 00:09:28.879
And I think, you know, there's something so funny about a New Yorker dropped into kind of rural Minnesota and just what that provokes in her.
我觉得，把一个纽约客空降到明尼苏达州的乡村，以及这在她身上引发的种种反应，真的非常有意思。

00:09:29.759 --> 00:09:30.799
But there's a lot going on.
不过这一集的信息量很大。

00:09:30.799 --> 00:09:36.279
There's like, there's like feminism classifying with like traditional stuff.
比如有女权主义和传统观念的碰撞。

00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:37.919
There's the size differences.
有体型差异带来的冲突。

00:09:37.919 --> 00:09:43.159
There's, I mean, her fear that she's gonna give birth to a 14 pound baby or whatever how much it was.
还有，我是说她害怕自己会生下一个14磅重的婴儿，管他到底有多重呢。

00:09:43.159 --> 00:09:44.159
It's so good.
太精彩了。

00:09:44.159 --> 00:09:45.000
It's so funny.
太搞笑了。

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:45.840
The turkey.
那只火鸡。

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:46.679
It's so good.
太棒了。

00:09:46.679 --> 00:09:52.000
Because every time you see that turkey, once you realize the turkey equals her future baby, the turkey's funny every time you see it.
因为每次你看到那只火鸡，一旦你意识到火鸡就等于她未来的孩子，你每次看到它都会觉得很好笑。

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:58.080
And it's sort of an act break shot where you're sort of like doing that thing of, you know, zooming in and pulling back at the same time and the bottom is falling out.
这有点像那种转场镜头，你一边在做那种拉近又拉远的效果，同时脚下的地面仿佛在塌陷。

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.480
It's like a Scorsese shot and she's the turkey.
就像马丁·斯科塞斯的镜头一样，而她就是那只火鸡。

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.173
just looming in the front of frame.
硕大无比地悬在画面正前方。

00:10:03.173 --> 00:10:04.250
It's so great.
太绝了。

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:20.640
And then from then on out, she's just always staring at that turkey thinking she's got to push one of those through her vagina any, you know, in a year and I wrote you this little day to send you in New York City.
从那以后，她就总是盯着那只火鸡，心想自己指不定哪天——比如一年后——也得把这么个东西从产道里挤出来，我还给你写了这么个小日子打发你在纽约的日子。

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.180
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:10:24.879 --> 00:10:28.085
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼（Alex Goldman），“超级修复”（Hyper-Fixed）播客的主持人。

00:10:28.085 --> 00:10:32.240
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们会收集听众的烦恼并试着帮他们解决。

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:35.360
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这样的烦恼：我30岁了，但害怕在纽约开车。

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:39.120
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者：为什么当我挂倒车档时，没办法调节汽车音响的音量？

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.679
We also solve non-car-related problems.
我们也会解决和汽车无关的问题。

00:10:41.679 --> 00:10:46.879
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有什么烦恼，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的那些隐藏在背后的运作机制。

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.279
That's the Hyper-Fixed podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的“超级修复”播客。

00:10:50.279 --> 00:10:53.639
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at hyperfixedpod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台收听，或者访问 hyperfixedpod.com。

00:11:05.639 --> 00:11:07.799
And now, back to the show.
现在，让我们回到节目中。

00:11:07.799 --> 00:11:10.840
Ted is always looking to the future as his salvation.
泰德（Ted）总是把未来当成自己的救赎。

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:15.340
Like Ted is always looking like, if I could just get these things to line up, the future's going to be glorious.
就像泰德总觉得，只要我能把这些事情理顺，未来就会一片辉煌。

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:17.059
I won't be struggling the way I'm saying.
我就不会像现在这样苦苦挣扎了。

00:11:17.059 --> 00:11:22.779
Whereas Lily kind of looks at this particular future, and she catastrophizes it, you know?
而莉莉看待这个特定的未来时，她把一切都灾难化了，你知道的？

00:11:22.779 --> 00:11:23.322
Yes, yes.
对，没错。

00:11:23.322 --> 00:11:25.192
She feels the walls closing in.
她感到四面楚歌。

00:11:25.192 --> 00:11:37.740
She feels like she's entered some sort of like weird like handmaid's tail adjacent to the universe where she'll have to birth these giant children for the good of the Republic, just pushing giant turkeys out.
她觉得自己仿佛进入了某种诡异的、类似《使女的故事》的平行宇宙，为了共和国的利益，她不得不生下这些巨大的孩子，就像硬生生挤出巨大的火鸡一样。

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:52.580
And her sense, her growing sense of kind of panic and displacement in this episode, it's so conjures what it feels like in a way for, I think it's a very relatable thing to have a partner and you go into their world.
她内心中那种不断滋生的恐慌和错位感在这集里表现得淋漓尽致，这真的勾勒出了一种感受——我觉得这非常有共鸣，那就是你拥有一个伴侣，然后你走进了他们的世界。

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:54.419
And now you're 100% in their world.
而现在你百分之百身处他们的世界。

00:11:54.419 --> 00:11:56.019
You're on their terms.
你得按他们的规矩来。

00:11:56.019 --> 00:11:58.179
I'm gonna say this very delicately.
我得非常委婉地说这件事。

00:11:58.179 --> 00:12:01.179
My wife is a really wonderful gourmet chef.
我妻子是一位非常优秀的美食大厨。

00:12:01.179 --> 00:12:05.100
She's Jewish, she's from Brooklyn, she just grew up with parents who were good cooks.
她是犹太人，来自布鲁克林，她的父母本身就很会做饭。

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.379
she joined my family, we are Irish cooks.
后来她加入了我的家庭，我们家都是做爱尔兰菜的厨子。

00:12:09.379 --> 00:12:12.940
It's not the same, there is more mayonnaise.
那可完全不一样，这里的蛋黄酱更多。

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:15.620
There's more mayonnaise involved in the cuisine.
我们的烹饪里加入了更多的蛋黄酱。

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.059
There's a lot more potatoes.
有更多的土豆。

00:12:17.059 --> 00:12:18.860
Things are cooked differently.
烹饪食物的方式也不一样。

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:27.500
There are vegetables that are green and there are vegetables that maybe have grown more brown in their preparation in some of my family's settings.
有些蔬菜是绿色的，而在我们家的一些饮食习惯里，有些蔬菜在烹饪后可能会变得更偏向褐色。

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:32.779
There's just, people like to cook things a lot more in the Irish world.
总之就是，在爱尔兰人的饮食世界里，人们就是喜欢把食物炖得更烂一点。

00:12:32.779 --> 00:12:35.941
And I say all this with love for both sides that debate.
我这话对辩论双方都充满着爱意。

00:12:35.941 --> 00:12:41.417
But a little bit of that went into this episode, but we went around the horn in the writers room.
但编剧室里确实借鉴了一点这种生活体验，我们在编剧室里轮流讨论过这个话题。

00:12:41.417 --> 00:12:50.788
And I remember everyone had some story like this of either they were the Mayo side, they were the seven layer Mayo salad half of their couple, or they went into that.
我记得每个人都有类似的故事，要么他们自己就是“蛋黄酱派”的，情侣中的一方就是那种会做七层蛋黄酱沙拉的人，要么他们曾经历过那种环境。

00:12:50.788 --> 00:12:58.522
We had a couple people that were from the Midwest around that table, and they very much had maybe not as insane as the seven layer salad.
我们桌上正好有几个来自中西部的人，他们对这种食物也深有体会，虽然可能没有七层沙拉那么疯狂。

00:12:58.522 --> 00:13:02.812
But there was definitely talk of like gummy bears, crunched up potato chips.
但大家确实聊过类似小熊软糖、压碎的薯片之类奇奇怪怪的搭配。

00:13:02.812 --> 00:13:06.482
Like the seven layer salad is actually not for anyone unfamiliar.
说到七层沙拉，其实对于不太了解的人来说。

00:13:06.482 --> 00:13:07.611
It exists in nature.
它在现实中是真实存在的。

00:13:07.611 --> 00:13:16.700
There's versions of this salad that are very much people in the writers room very much remembered growing up eating iterations of slightly toned down iterations.
这种沙拉确实存在各种版本，编剧室里的很多人都清楚地记得自己从小到大吃过各种改良版的、稍微没那么极端的版本。

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:21.349
Everyone has a normal childhood in as much as it's the only childhood you know.
每个人都有一个觉得正常的童年，因为那是你唯一知道的童年。

00:13:21.349 --> 00:13:28.235
Yeah, that's the one you're like, I don't know, was it it's only when you get older and you start actually comparing.
对啊，那就是你会觉得——我不知道，只有当你长大了，开始真正去作对比的时候。

00:13:28.235 --> 00:13:30.413
And you realize, Oh, that was insane.
你才会意识到，天哪，那太疯狂了。

00:13:30.413 --> 00:13:32.120
Like, why are we eating that?
比如，我们当时为什么要吃那个？

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:33.944
Like that was that was bonkers.
简直不可理喻。

00:13:33.944 --> 00:13:36.063
But everyone has a seven year salad.
但每个人都有自己的“七层沙拉”。

00:13:36.063 --> 00:13:37.535
Do you have that in Ohio?
你们俄亥俄州有这东西吗？

00:13:37.535 --> 00:13:41.007
Do you do you have one versions of that growing up in Ohio?
你在俄亥俄州长大时，吃过类似版本的食物吗？

00:13:41.007 --> 00:13:49.365
We definitely had like my sisters and I joke about certain meals my mom made that were part of the rotation that were like, Oh, what was that?
我们绝对有——我和我姐姐们经常开玩笑拿我妈常做的某些家常菜开涮，比如，天哪，那到底是什么鬼？

00:13:49.365 --> 00:13:53.190
I mean, there was I remember there was this dish that we kind of.
我是说，我记得有这么一道菜，我们……

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:54.132
Hi, Mrs. Wagner.
嗨，瓦格纳夫人（Mrs. Wagner）。

00:13:54.132 --> 00:14:03.019
Hi, but it was called chicken It was chicken devan that had like a real Manaz sauce and there was broccoli, chicken, and like lemony Manaz-y something.
嗨，不过那道菜叫鸡肉——那是德凡鸡（chicken devan），里面有真正的蛋黄酱酱汁，还有西兰花、鸡肉，以及某种带点柠檬味和蛋黄酱味的东西。

00:14:03.019 --> 00:14:13.139
But, you know, there was, we were from the land of, you know, the green bean mushroom salad at Thanksgiving with crispy onion rings on top.
但是呢，你知道的，我们来自那个独特的故乡——感恩节必有青豆蘑菇沙拉，上面还铺着脆洋葱圈。

00:14:13.139 --> 00:14:14.700
That's delicious, I will defend that.
那个很好吃，我要为它辩护。

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:15.940
There's nothing wrong with that.
那没有任何问题。

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:17.139
That's on its texture, right?
关键在于它的口感，对吧？

00:14:17.139 --> 00:14:17.980
A little crushy on those.
上面那些酥脆的东西。

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:19.620
I was people with that, I know they do.
我知道有人很喜欢那个，我懂的。

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:20.740
Absolutely.
绝对的。

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:21.580
Yeah.
对。

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:33.700
But there are those things about your childhood, whether it's something with food or even basque ice ball, is like a thing, which I'm curious like where that came from.
但你的童年里总会有那么一些食物，无论是某种食物还是甚至像巴斯克冰淇淋球（basque ice ball）这种东西——这也是个神奇的存在，我很好奇这到底是从哪来的。

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:42.129
But there's something so funny about the weirdness of a family that to the family is like utterly normal.
不过，一个家庭的那些怪癖对他们自己来说完全是司空见惯的，这一点真的非常有意思。

00:14:44.009 --> 00:14:46.690
But to an outsider, you're like, what are you eating?
但对一个外人来说，你会想：你们到底在吃什么？

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:47.610
Like, what are you doing?
天哪，你们在搞什么鬼？

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:51.690
This is not how life is supposed to be lived or meals are supposed to be had.
生活或吃饭根本不应该是这个样子的。

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:54.289
Yeah, I really like the moment late in the episode.
对，我真的很喜欢这一集后半段的那个时刻。

00:14:54.289 --> 00:14:59.649
I mean, first of all, just like they're seeing Jason and Allie seen in the prison cell is great.
我是说，首先，杰森（Jason）和阿莉（Allie）在牢房里的那场戏就太棒了。

00:14:59.649 --> 00:15:00.490
Oh, so good.
哦，太精彩了。

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:01.330
So good.
太精彩了。

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:02.409
And it's sort of the, that's so great.
这真的是，太棒了。

00:15:02.409 --> 00:15:04.049
They're so lovable and real.
他们太讨人喜欢了，而且很真实。

00:15:04.049 --> 00:15:07.409
And they're just, it's yet another moment where you just fall in love with that couple.
而且这就是又一个让你对那对夫妻深陷爱河的瞬间。

00:15:07.409 --> 00:15:16.090
And they, I just, his moment, the moment she said she might be pregnant, his almost immediate response is, we can never let one of our children play basketball.
而且他们，我就是，他的那个时刻，当她说她可能怀孕时，他几乎是不假思索地回答说，我们绝不能让我们的孩子打篮球。

00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:22.049
It's a death trap, but he sees it through like outside eyes for the first time in that moment.
那简直是个死亡陷阱，但在那个瞬间，他第一次像个局外人一样看清了这一点。

00:15:22.049 --> 00:15:25.049
And then we've all had that sort of moment of clarity, right?
然后我们都有过那种醍醐灌顶的时刻，对吧？

00:15:25.049 --> 00:15:28.289
Where you look back at your childhood, you go, oh, that was fucking weird.
当你回首童年时，你会想，天哪，那真他妈太诡异了。

00:15:28.289 --> 00:15:29.289
That was really weird.
真的太诡异了。

00:15:29.289 --> 00:15:32.529
What we used to do or what we were allowed to do.
我们以前做的事，或者我们被允许做的事。

00:15:32.529 --> 00:15:45.690
Sometimes just with my children now, raising kids in 2025, I think of like, my friends and I, when we were nine, like my daughter's nine, my parents wouldn't know where we were for 16 hours because we were in the woods blowing shit up or whatever we're doing.
有时候，就拿我现在抚养孩子来说，在2025年养育孩子，我会想起我和朋友们九岁的时候，比如我女儿现在九岁，我父母有16个小时根本不知道我们在哪里，因为我们在树林里炸这炸那或者干别的什么。

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.929
and like dirt bikes off of jumps next to a cliff.
还有像在悬崖边的跳台上骑越野摩托车。

00:15:48.929 --> 00:15:51.929
Like you're just like, what in God's name were we allowed to do this?
你会忍不住想，天哪，我们当时怎么被允许做这些事的？

00:15:51.929 --> 00:15:56.009
I'm getting a little bit into like old guide material now of like kids that are different.
我现在有点像是在扯到老一辈的话题了，比如那些与众不同的孩子们。

00:15:56.009 --> 00:16:00.009
Which is the seeing that through fresh eyes is a very sobering moment.
用全新的视角看待这一切，是一个非常让人清醒的时刻。

00:16:00.009 --> 00:16:00.850
Ready to go?
准备好了吗？

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:01.690
Oh, that's weird.
哦，那太奇怪了。

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:02.529
That's weird that my family did.
我家人做过的事真奇怪。

00:16:02.529 --> 00:16:04.929
Have we talked about the podcast Heavy Weight?
我们聊过播客节目《重担》吗？

00:16:04.929 --> 00:16:06.610
Do you know Heavy Weight?
你知道《重担》吗？

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:07.450
No, I don't think so.
不知道，我不觉得我知道。

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:08.610
It's an amazing podcast.
这是一个极其精彩的播客。

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:11.090
This guy, I think Jonathan Goldstein's name hosts it.
这个主持人好像叫乔纳森·戈德斯坦。

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:15.009
But it's basically like he talks to people who are carrying a heavy weight.
但基本上就是他会和那些背负着沉重心理负担的人交谈。

00:16:15.009 --> 00:16:19.210
like the kinds of things that keep you up at three in the morning, like, why did I do that thing?
